Don't write every class. Write:

- **Interfaces** for the strategy / extension points.
- **The 1-2 methods that drive the flow** — `parkingLot.park()`, `feeStrategy.calculate()`.
- **Enums** if they clarify (`SpotType`, `VehicleType`, `OrderStatus`).

```java
public interface FeeStrategy {
    Money calculate(Ticket ticket, Instant exitTime);
}

public class HourlyFeeStrategy implements FeeStrategy {
    private final Map<SpotType, Money> hourlyRates;

    @Override
    public Money calculate(Ticket t, Instant exitTime) {
        long hours = Duration.between(t.entryTime(), exitTime).toHours();
        if (hours == 0) hours = 1; // partial hour rounds up
        return hourlyRates.get(t.spot().type()).times(hours);
    }
}

public class ParkingLot {
    private final List<Floor> floors;
    private final FeeStrategy feeStrategy;
    private final TicketRepository tickets;

    public Ticket park(Vehicle v) {
        Spot s = findSpotFor(v).orElseThrow(LotFullException::new);
        s.assign(v);
        Ticket t = new Ticket(TicketId.next(), v, s, Instant.now());
        tickets.save(t);
        return t;
    }
}
```

**Skip:** getters, setters, constructors, `toString`, exception class bodies. Say 'standard boilerplate' and move on.

**One class, one reason to change.**

**Violation:**
```java
class Order {
    void place();
    void calculateTax();           // tax rules change for tax reasons
    void sendConfirmationEmail();  // email format changes for marketing reasons
    void persistToDb();            // schema changes for DB reasons
    void exportToCsv();            // export format changes for reporting reasons
}
```
Four different reasons to change — four different teams editing the same file.

**Fix — split by reason to change:**
```java
class Order { /* state, place(), cancel() */ }
class TaxCalculator { Money taxFor(Order o); }
class OrderConfirmationMailer { void send(Order o); }
class OrderRepository { void save(Order o); }
class OrderExporter { String toCsv(Order o); }
```

Now each class changes for one reason. The use case orchestrates them.

**Open for extension, closed for modification.**

New behavior should be addable without editing existing classes. The mechanism: an **interface + new implementation**.

**Violation — every new payment method touches `PaymentProcessor`:**
```java
class PaymentProcessor {
    void pay(Order o, String method) {
        if (method.equals("credit_card")) { /* ... */ }
        else if (method.equals("paypal"))  { /* ... */ }
        else if (method.equals("upi"))     { /* ... */ }   // added
        else if (method.equals("crypto"))  { /* ... */ }   // added
    }
}
```

**Fix — Strategy interface:**
```java
public interface PaymentMethod {
    PaymentResult charge(Money amount);
}
class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentMethod { ... }
class PaypalPayment implements PaymentMethod { ... }
class UpiPayment implements PaymentMethod { ... }       // new — no PaymentProcessor edit
```

Now adding a payment method is a new file, never an edit to existing code. Critical when the existing code has dozens of dependents and tests.

**Subclasses must honor the parent's contract.** Anywhere `Parent` is expected, a `Child` instance must work correctly without surprise.

**Classic violation — Square extends Rectangle:**
```java
class Rectangle {
    void setWidth(int w);
    void setHeight(int h);
}
class Square extends Rectangle {
    void setWidth(int w)  { super.setWidth(w);  super.setHeight(w); }   // forces height
    void setHeight(int h) { super.setWidth(h);  super.setHeight(h); }   // forces width
}

void resize(Rectangle r) {
    r.setWidth(5);
    r.setHeight(10);
    assert r.area() == 50;   // ❌ fails for Square (returns 100)
}
```

`Square` violates LSP because it breaks the expectation that width and height vary independently.

**Fix — don't model square as a rectangle. Model both as `Shape`:**
```java
interface Shape { int area(); }
class Rectangle(int w, int h) implements Shape { ... }
class Square(int side)         implements Shape { ... }
```

**Practical heuristic:** if a subclass overrides a method to throw `UnsupportedOperationException` or behaves differently from documented expectations, you've violated LSP — the inheritance is wrong.

**Don't force clients to depend on methods they don't use.** Many small role-specific interfaces beat one fat interface.

**Violation:**
```java
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
    void sleep();
}
class Robot implements Worker {
    void work() { ... }
    void eat()  { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }   // ❌
    void sleep(){ throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }   // ❌
}
```

**Fix — split by role:**
```java
interface Workable { void work(); }
interface Eatable  { void eat(); }
interface Sleepable{ void sleep(); }

class Human implements Workable, Eatable, Sleepable { ... }
class Robot implements Workable { ... }
```

**LLD application:** keep your repository / gateway / strategy interfaces focused. A `UserRepository` with 30 methods is hard to mock and signals that some methods belong elsewhere. A small `UserFinder`, `UserUpdater`, `UserDeleter` split is often cleaner.

**Depend on abstractions, not concretions.** High-level modules and low-level modules both depend on interfaces.

**Violation:**
```java
class OrderService {
    private MySqlOrderRepository repo = new MySqlOrderRepository();   // concrete
    private SmtpEmailSender mailer  = new SmtpEmailSender();          // concrete
}
```
You can't unit-test without MySQL and an SMTP server. You can't swap the database. You can't decorate the mailer with retries.

**Fix:**
```java
interface OrderRepository { ... }
interface EmailSender    { ... }

class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;
    private final EmailSender     mailer;
    OrderService(OrderRepository repo, EmailSender mailer) { ... }   // constructor injection
}
```

`OrderService` depends on the interfaces. Adapters (`MySqlOrderRepository`, `SmtpEmailSender`) implement them. In tests, fakes are passed in. In production, Spring wires the real ones via `@Configuration` or `@Component`.

**Constructor injection** is the canonical Java mechanism. Avoid field injection (hides dependencies, breaks immutability).
